                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "np.rolling_buffer keeps the last N things that arrived, as one array.\n\nIt works across TIME rather than within an array. Feed it a value or a small  array on every frame and it hands back the most recent N of them stacked  together - which is how a stream becomes something you can take a mean of,  plot as a history, or run a filter across. Almost anything you want to know  about \"the recent past\" starts here.\n\nNEWEST FIRST:\nRow 0 of the output is the sample that just arrived; the last row is the  oldest still kept. A plot of it scrolls from left to right, with now at the  left edge.\n\nTHE SHAPE:\nThe arguments are the buffer's shape. The first is how many samples to keep; \nany after it are the shape of each sample. np.rolling_buffer 60 keeps 60  single numbers; np.rolling_buffer 60 3 keeps 60 three-value samples, as a 60  by 3 array. With no arguments it keeps 16 single numbers.\n\nA sample of the wrong shape is ignored without complaint - if nothing comes  out, check what is going in. A sample with one extra leading axis of size 1  (a 1 by 3 array into a buffer of threes) is accepted.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.rolling_buffer <length> <sample shape...>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.rolling_buffer 60 3\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe latest sample. Receiving it adds it to the buffer and sends the buffer.\n\nreset:\nEmpties the buffer and sends the empty array, so displays clear at once.\n\nOUTPUTS: \n\nout:\nThe buffer's contents, newest first. Each send is a fresh array, so  anything downstream that keeps one sees it stay as it was.\n\nIT GROWS BEFORE IT ROLLS:\nA rolling buffer only reports what it has actually collected, so a freshly  reset one is short until it fills: 1 row, then 2, up to N. That is  deliberate - it avoids feeding you a stretch of zeros that never happened -  but it does mean the output's length changes for the first N samples.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.cumsum and np.diff run along the buffer once you have one."
        },
